  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure centered over the Mid Atlantic will drift east\ntonight through Sunday morning. Mostly fair weather is expected\nthrough Sunday morning before a cold front approaches the\nInternational Border Sunday afternoon. This front will bring\nchances for showers and thunderstorms Sunday afternoon into the\nevening across the mountains into portions of central Maine. A\ndry airmass in place will limit instability with MU CAPE\ngenerally less than 1000 J/kg. Therefore, the threat for severe\nstorms is low while deep inverted V low level profiles may allow\nfor gusty outflow winds with any stronger cells. Areas south of\nthe mountains and southwest of the Capital District of Maine\nlikely remain dry into sunset. Southwest winds will increase\nthrough the morning ahead of the front with gusts 15 to 20 mph\nby early afternoon. These winds will allow for mixing to around\n850 mb where temperatures will be close to +15C leading to highs\nsouth of the mountains into the mid 80s. North of the mountains\nhighs will be in the 70s.\n\nThe cold front will continue to press southward Sunday night\nwith shower chances expanding south towards the coast the first\nhalf of Sunday night. Shower chances diminish the second half of\nthe night as the front pushes offshore. High pressure then\nbuilds in behind the front for fair weather on Monday with highs\nin the 70s to low 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFast WNW 500 mb flow overspreads the Northeast U.S., on the \nperiphery of a strong ridge to our south. It will be a favorable \nsetup for disturbances to ride the ridge and bring some rain chances \nto New England. Guidance has been all over the place with the timing \nof these waves, but beginning to see agreement in Tuesday, then \nagain on Wednesday PM-Thursday, as the two windows of opportunity \nfor more widespread rainfall. Some drier stretches are expected \noutside of these two windows, but really cannot rule out a shower \nfrom popping up in this ring of fire type pattern at any time \nthrough next week.\n\nThere are certainly signals for multiple rounds of heavy rainfall in \nthis pattern, but the spatial extent and whether it is centered over \nour forecast area remain uncertain. Ensemble guidance currently \nshows a near 50% chance of exceeding 1\" QPF for Tuesday-Friday, \nwhile that number drops to 10-20% for >2\". In summary, there is \ncertainly the potential for some much needed rainfall, but generally \nnot concerned about excessive rainfall at this time, likely owing to \na rather progressive nature to the flow pattern.\n\nTemperatures are also lower confidence primarily because of a warm \nfront/quasi-stationary front that sets up in the vicinity. ECMWF/NAM \nbrings the warm sector north into SW NH on Tuesday, while other \nguidance keeps it well south of the forecast area. Am skeptical on a \nnorthern outcome especially since morning rain/convection looks to \nbe involved. Shifting to Wednesday-Thursday, all guidance keeps us \nnorth of the front. This all is likely to result in cooler highs in \nthe 60s-70s, but mild/muggy nights with dew points in the upper \n50s/low 60s the floor for low temperatures.",
